module gunbc.runner_slot_allocation

import std.types { Int, String, List, Bool }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count, HardwareThreadCount, hardware_thread_count, hardware_thread_count_value }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal, SingleAuthority, Scaffold }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import product.placement_supply { HostIdentity }
import gunbc.fleet_intent_network {
  operator_host_srv1, operator_host_srv2, operator_host_srv3, operator_host_srv4,
}
import gunbc.runner_host_deploy { srv4_runner_host_deploy }
import gunbc.ci_failure_class { FloorOutcome, NoFailure, FloorFailed, Structural, floor_outcome_blocks_merge }

type RunnerSlotLabels {
  github_labels: List<String>
}

type RunnerSlotDesired {
  memory_max: ByteSize
  memory_swap_max: ByteSize
  memory_high: ByteSize
  tasks_max: Int
  cpu_weight: Int
  labels: RunnerSlotLabels
}

type HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
  host: HostIdentity
  slots_per_host: Int
  cores_per_slot: HardwareThreadCount
  runner_slice_cap: ByteSize
  per_slot: RunnerSlotDesired
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_authority_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for fleet runner-slot desired allocation (operator rulings 2026-07-10 superseding the 2026-07-05 brief; MemoryHigh-emit + swap-provisioning 2026-07-11; srv3 host-standup converge 2026-07-21; PER-HOST WIDTH + 14GiB slot 2026-08-05). Fleet-uniform per-slot row: 8 cores · 14GiB MemoryMax · 13GiB MemoryHigh (the authored max−1GiB relation, its own emitted knob per the 2026-07-11 ruling — a hit at 14GiB spills to swap after throttling at 13) · 32GiB MemorySwapMax · TasksMax 512 · cpu_weight 100. PER-HOST width: MEMORY admits 5/5/7/7 for srv1/srv2/srv3/srv4, and the COMMITTED width is the minimum over resolved axes, so srv4 commits 5 under its declared disk cap (width_is_a_minimum_over_axes_note) and the fleet commits 22, not 24. GUARANTEED mode is now STRUCTURAL rather than checked — runner_slice_cap is DERIVED as slots × MemoryMax instead of being a second literal, so Σ per-slot MemoryMax ≤ slice_cap holds by construction and an oversubscribed slice has no representation here (DESIGN 5 construction-over-validation; the prior 80GiB literal was kept coherent with 5 × 16GiB by hand, which the old note admitted when it called them 'coherent by construction at these values' — a coincidence maintained by an author, not a derivation). The real remaining constraint is CONSERVATION against host RAM, which is not this module's to check: gunbc.ci_runner_placement holds it, now against OS-usable RAM with the per-host session slice and per-host fixed overhead charged. Per-axis targets are operator-set; never reconcile this row toward live readback (live is not automatically truth — the 32-vs-0 swap fork existed precisely because the apply was assumed rather than readback-proven). Downstream: gunbc.ci_floor_measurement (declared vs live cap fork), gunbc.ci_runner_placement, gunbc.host_converge, gunbc.host_standup (prefix:host-converge + P1:runner-deploy-slot), gunbc.runner_slot_enforcement, gunbc.fleet_show_effective_read. ctrl applies host-side; gunbc emits converge — never apply from this module."
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_memory_max_ruling_note: String = "WHY 14GiB AND NOT 16, measured rather than preferred (operator ruling 2026-08-05). 186 floor-job cgroup peaks from 2026-08-05 plus five sampled days back to 2026-07-16, read from the [calibration] floor_peak_post receipts the ci job emits: successful runs peak at p50 10.32GiB and max 10.96GiB, and NOT ONE run in the sample reached the 16GiB MemoryMax. Four of 186 reached 15.0GiB, which is the 15GiB MemoryHigh exactly — those are throttle pins, censored lower bounds rather than demand, and they are why the cap does not fall further.\n\nTHE FLOOR UNDER IT IS ALREADY MEASURED AND IT IS 12GiB: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget in this module, enforced by claim_executor as an arm-time refusal. MemoryHigh is what the run-time governor arms against, so MemoryHigh is the axis that number constrains — 13GiB leaves exactly 1GiB of margin over it, and clears the worst p90 in the three-week sample (12.94GiB on 2026-07-30). Going to 13/12 to buy an eighth slot on the managed hosts would put the throttle line ON the measured refusal floor with zero margin, and the 2026-07-30 distribution says that day would have spent most of its runs in swap.\n\nWHAT MOVING 16 TO 14 IS NOT: it is not oversubscription and does not touch the guaranteed-mode property. Every slot still owns its ceiling outright. The ceilings are simply sized closer to what the workload was measured to want, which is what freed the width."

data gunbc_runner_slot_width_ruling_note: String = "WHY srv3/srv4 GET 7 AND srv1/srv2 STAY AT 5 — the width difference is DERIVED from two per-host facts, not granted by preference. First, sessions: gunbc.session_residency host_session_residency places interactive session containers on srv1 and srv2 and on neither managed host, so srv1/srv2 owe a sessions reservation out of the same host RAM and srv3/srv4 do not. Second, fixed overhead: gunbc.ci_floor_measurement carries a measured upper bound for the managed hosts and an operator allowance for the legacy pair, which differ by 10.4GiB per host.\n\nRun the conservation arithmetic with both charged and the MEMORY admissions fall out — srv3/srv4 admit 7 slots at 14GiB and refuse 8 (an eighth would need 112GiB against ~111.6GiB available, over by less than a gibibyte), srv1/srv2 admit 5 and refuse 6. Neither number is authored here as a target; both are the largest width gunbc.ci_runner_placement's wall will pass, and if either per-host fact is corrected the admissible width moves with it.\n\nMEMORY ADMISSION IS NOT THE COMMITMENT. This note explains one axis; the CANDIDATE width is the minimum over every resolved axis and is derived in candidate_width_under_current_topology. srv4 is the case where the axes differ — memory admits 7, its declared disk cap admits 5, and its candidate is 5. What the fleet can actually be made to run is a third and smaller number again (three_widths_are_three_facts_note): every host's actuatable width is 5 today, because no activation receipt exists.\n\nTHE SESSIONS FACT IS RESIDENCY, AND SAYING SO HERE IS LOAD-BEARING. An earlier revision of this note derived the same widths from gunbc.build_cache_instance instead — whether a host carried an InteractiveSessionCompiles cache instance — which merely CORRELATED with sessions living there. That proxy is the defect this change removes, and gunbc.session_residency why_this_is_not_derived_from_the_build_cache_roster_note records how close it came: the session cache on srv1 and srv2 was found provably dead on 2026-08-06 and deleted, so dropping its now-meaningless roster rows would have stopped charging both hosts their reservation and admitted 7 slots each on a wall returning true. A note still reasoning from the roster would invite exactly that edit, which is why it is corrected rather than left as harmless prose.\n\nTHE FLEET-UNIFORM ROW IS WHY THIS COULD NOT BE EXPRESSED BEFORE. gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation took a host and then read three globals, so 'this host is different' had nowhere to live and the only way to raise srv3/srv4 was to raise all four — which on srv1/srv2 would have overcommitted by 17GiB against a wall that could not see the sessions it was overcommitting against."

fn gunbc_runner_slot_desired() -> RunnerSlotDesired {
  RunnerSlotDesired {
    memory_max: byte_size(15032385536),
    memory_swap_max: byte_size(34359738368),
    memory_high: byte_size(13958643712),
    tasks_max: 512,
    cpu_weight: 100,
    labels: RunnerSlotLabels { github_labels: ["self-hosted", "linux", "arm64", "gunbc-heavy"] }
  }
}

data unknown_host_width_refuses_note: String = "Unknown hosts refuse rather than defaulting. A width is a claim about a specific machine's RAM, and handing an unclassified host the managed pair's 7 would be the same borrowed-premise failure the fixed-overhead fallback carries (gunbc.ci_floor_measurement gunbc_ci_host_fixed_overhead_unknown_host_note) — worse here, because width multiplies the per-slot ceiling. Zero slots is the fail-closed answer on ALL THREE widths — candidate, actuatable and observed each return zero for a host they do not name — and gunbc.ci_runner_placement's any_unfit arm already refuses a plan that admits zero runners on an enrolled host, so an unclassified host produces a located PlanUnsound rather than a silent five."

data width_is_a_minimum_over_axes_note: String = "A HOST'S WIDTH IS THE MINIMUM OVER ITS CONSTRAINTS, and until this row existed the function below returned the MEMORY verdict under a name that read as the commitment. That is the defect that made srv4 report 7 while every other authority in the tree said 5: gunbc.runner_host_deploy srv4_runner_count_disk_cap_note pins it at 5 on a declared disk constraint, gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4_admission_note said in prose that slots 06/07 must not be provisioned on the solver's word, and the roster nevertheless published 7 — so the disagreement lived between a number and a paragraph, where no consumer can see it.\n\nCollapsing 'one constraint admits this width' into 'this host should run this width' is the same conflation gunbc.ci_floor_measurement refuses for overhead: an axis nobody resolved is not an axis that permits everything. So memory admission and disk admission are separate answers, and the committed width is their minimum — which makes srv4's 5 a DERIVATION rather than a caveat, and moves it the moment either axis moves.\n\nHONEST RESIDUAL, because this is only two axes: a host with no declared disk cap has its disk UNCONSTRAINED here, not measured, and that is a fail-open on the disk axis for srv1-3. CORRECTION, and it matters because the sentence it replaces was false in the one place it needed to be true: an earlier revision said the arm was mere status quo because \"those three are not widened by this change\". srv3 IS widened by this change, from five to seven, and it is the ONLY host being widened — so the unmeasured disk axis is not inherited debt for srv3, it is an axis dropped out of the minimum for exactly the host whose width is moving. That is why DiskWidthUnconstrained carries a reason string rather than being an absence, and why srv3 owes a disk observation before anything is actuated on it. Dissolve-on is the one already named on srv4_runner_count_disk_cap_note: a per-host disk-headroom term folded into runner_deployment_plan, at which point both hosts' disk widths are derived and this arm disappears."

fn host_memory_admitted_width(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv1 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv2 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv3 as String) {
    7
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv4 as String) {
    7
  } else {
    0
  }
}

type HostDiskWidthConstraint
  = DiskAdmitsWidth { slots: Int, provenance: String }
  | DiskWidthUnconstrained { reason: String }

fn host_disk_admitted_width(host: HostIdentity) -> HostDiskWidthConstraint {
  if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv4 as String) {
    DiskAdmitsWidth {
      slots: srv4_runner_host_deploy.runner_count,
      provenance: "gunbc.runner_host_deploy srv4_runner_host_deploy runner_count, the declared cap recorded in srv4_runner_count_disk_cap_note: a 500GB Samsung 970 EVO against 2TB on srv1-3, with every ephemeral runner holding a _work tree plus caches"
    }
  } else {
    DiskWidthUnconstrained {
      reason: "no per-host disk cap is declared for this host and no disk-headroom term is modeled, so its disk admits no stated width and does not lower the minimum: an unmeasured axis, not a measured all-clear"
    }
  }
}

data three_widths_are_three_facts_note: String = "ONE NUMBER WAS CARRYING THREE DIFFERENT CLAIMS, and the strongest of them was the one being published. A single committed width answered at once what the constraints ADMIT, what the fleet can be MADE to run today, and what is ACTUALLY RUNNING — and those three are 5/5/7/5, 5/5/5/5 and 5/5/5/5, so reporting the first as the commitment overstated the fleet by two runners that no code path can create (operator ruling, 2026-08-06).\n\nWHY THE GAP IS NOT PAPERWORK. Nothing in this repository creates or activates a slot: host_converge_realize implements PerSlotMemoryCap and returns KnobUnimplemented for RunnerWidth, and runner activation additionally requires a grounded managed-cache receipt and a bound compile-pool receipt that gunbc.host_compile_pool reports as not-ready for every host under CompilePoolInRunnerSlots. So the seventh srv3 slot is not merely unprovisioned, it is unreachable through the modeled path, and a width that claims it is a claim about a machine no one has changed.\n\nAND THE CANDIDATE IS NOT MERELY WAITING ON LABOUR. Flipping to a managed compile pool adds a SIBLING RAM claim sized from jobserver tokens: seven slots at 14GiB is 98GiB and the cited 62-token derivation is a further 108.5GiB, which does not fit in ~125GiB before overhead or headroom. That sum is very likely double-counting — the per-slot measurement was taken with compiles running INSIDE the slots, so their residency is already in the 14GiB — and that is exactly the point: the two claims have never been decomposed, so the candidate seven is unproven in both directions rather than merely unscheduled.\n\nTHE RULE THIS ENCODES: an operator-declared evidence grade may grant CANDIDATE capacity and may not grant LIVE capacity. srv3 and srv4 are session-free by declaration rather than observation (gunbc.session_residency), which is good enough to keep seven as a candidate and not good enough to actuate on. Only a grounded activation receipt advances actuatable_desired_width."

fn candidate_width_under_current_topology(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  let by_memory = host_memory_admitted_width(host: host)
  match host_disk_admitted_width(host: host) {
    DiskAdmitsWidth { slots: d, provenance: _ } => if d < by_memory { d } else { by_memory }
    DiskWidthUnconstrained { reason: _ } => by_memory
  }
}

type WidthActuationStanding
  = ActuationGrounded { receipt: String }
  | ActuationUngrounded { reason: String }

data actuation_standing_is_ungrounded_fleet_wide_note: String = "EVERY HOST IS UNGROUNDED TODAY, and this function is written as a per-host question rather than a fleet-wide constant so that srv3 can be advanced alone when its receipt exists, without the arm becoming a place to assert readiness. It is the seam the srv3 capacity lane closes.\n\nUngrounded is a REFUSAL TO ADVANCE, not a claim that the host is broken: it says no receipt proves this host can be taken past what is already observed running on it, so the actuatable width falls back to the observed one. That is the conservative direction on the axis where being wrong is unrecoverable — an actuatable width above what a machine can hold is an OOM, and an actuatable width equal to what is already running cannot be."

fn width_actuation_standing(host: HostIdentity) -> WidthActuationStanding {
  ActuationUngrounded {
    reason: concat("no grounded activation receipt exists for any host: runner activation requires a managed build-cache receipt and a bound compile-pool receipt, and gunbc.host_compile_pool reports not-ready under the live CompilePoolInRunnerSlots topology, so no slot beyond the observed live width can be reached through the modeled path. Host: ", host)
  }
}

data observed_live_width_note: String = "WHAT IS ACTUALLY RUNNING, carried as an observation with a date because it is the only one of the three widths that is a fact about the world rather than a verdict about a model. Five ephemeral runner units per host on all four hosts, evenly loaded, visible in any run's job census; srv4 has carried exactly this since 2026-07-22.\n\nIt is deliberately NOT derived from the declared width. Deriving it would make the observation agree with the model by construction and destroy the only signal that says whether an intent was ever applied — which is the same reason gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_authority_disposition forbids reconciling declared rows toward live readback: the two must be able to disagree, in both directions, or neither is evidence."

fn observed_live_width(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv1 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv2 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv3 as String) {
    5
  } else if (host as String) == (operator_host_srv4 as String) {
    5
  } else {
    0
  }
}

fn actuatable_desired_width(host: HostIdentity) -> Int {
  match width_actuation_standing(host: host) {
    ActuationGrounded { receipt: _ } => candidate_width_under_current_topology(host: host)
    ActuationUngrounded { reason: _ } => observed_live_width(host: host)
  }
}

data consumers_read_the_actuatable_width_note: String = "THE DEPLOY AND CONVERGE PATHS READ actuatable_desired_width, because every one of them is answering 'what should exist on this host right now' rather than 'what could this host hold'. gunbc_runner_slice_cap_declared sizes the slice a host's units actually occupy; gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation is what gunbc.host_converge cross-checks against LIVE unit enumeration, refusing the host on a mismatch.\n\nThat cross-check is why routing the candidate here was a live defect and not only an overstatement: with srv3 declared at seven and five units on the box, `gunbc converge` refused srv3 outright — the model told the converge path to expect a host that nobody had built, and the refusal was correct behaviour serving a false premise. Reading the actuatable width restores agreement between declared and live without anyone editing a number toward the machine, which the authority disposition forbids.\n\nThe candidate stays reachable and stays seven. It is what a solver and the capacity lane consume, and it is the number that moves first when srv3's observations land."

fn gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot() -> HardwareThreadCount {
  hardware_thread_count(count: 8)
}

fn gunbc_runner_slice_cap_declared(host: HostIdentity) -> ByteSize {
  gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(
    slots: actuatable_desired_width(host: host),
    per_slot: gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max
  )
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(slots: Int, per_slot: ByteSize) -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(byte_size_count(b: per_slot) * slots)
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice(slots: Int, per_slot: ByteSize, slice_cap: ByteSize) -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: gunbc_runner_slot_demand_sum_memory_max(slots: slots, per_slot: per_slot)) <=
    byte_size_count(b: slice_cap)
}

fn gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(host: HostIdentity) -> HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
  let per_slot = gunbc_runner_slot_desired()
  let slots = actuatable_desired_width(host: host)
  HostRunnerSlotAllocation {
    host: host,
    slots_per_host: slots,
    cores_per_slot: gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot(),
    runner_slice_cap: gunbc_runner_slice_cap_declared(host: host),
    per_slot: per_slot
  }
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv1: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv1
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv2: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv2
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv3: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv3
)

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4_admission_note: String = "srv4 JOINS THE COMMITTED ROSTER, and its absence until now was a live fork rather than an oversight. srv4 has been carrying fleet work since 2026-07-22 — five slots srv4-01..05, evenly loaded, visible in any run's job census — while this roster listed srv1/srv2/srv3 only. So the host that OOM-killed live CI for being outside the model (gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps section C) was re-enrolled in gunbc.fleet_intent and in the compute offers, and then left out of the one authority that says what its slots are supposed to be. Its per-slot caps existed on the box as an interim hand-apply, legitimate at the time only because srv4 and srv1 are the same board at the same RAM, which made the derived values EQUAL rather than transplanted.\n\nEqual-by-coincidence is exactly what stops being true here: srv4's MEMORY admission moves to 7 while srv1 stays at 5, so a roster that omits srv4 no longer merely lags the fleet, it disagrees with it. That memory difference is derived from srv4 hosting no interactive sessions, per gunbc_runner_slot_width_ruling_note.\n\nSRV4 NEVERTHELESS COMMITS 5, AND IT IS NOW DERIVED RATHER THAN WARNED ABOUT. An earlier revision of this note said in prose that slots 06 and 07 must not be provisioned on the solver's word while the single published width handed 7 to every consumer — a paragraph disagreeing with a number, which is the weaker half losing. host_disk_admitted_width now reads the declared cap on srv4_runner_host_deploy and the candidate is the minimum of the two axes, so srv4's candidate is 5 by construction. When the disk term is modeled and the cap rises, the width rises with it and no note needs editing.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED HERE: srv4's NVMe is a 500GB Samsung 970 EVO against 2TB on srv1-3, and gunbc.runner_host_deploy srv4_runner_count_disk_cap_note records disk as its binding constraint at 5 runners. That note's arithmetic is stale — it cites a RAM-derived 10 from the retired 8GiB×10 shape — but its FACT is not, and 7 ephemeral _work trees on a 500GB disk is a real question this change does not answer. The conservation wall models RAM only; a per-host disk-headroom term is the named dissolution trigger on that note, and it is the gate on srv4 actually running the seventh slot."

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4: HostRunnerSlotAllocation = gunbc_committed_host_runner_slot_allocation(
  host: operator_host_srv4
)

fn gunbc_committed_runner_slot_allocations() -> List<HostRunnerSlotAllocation> {
  [
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv1,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv2,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv3,
    gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_srv4,
  ]
}

fn gunbc_runner_slot_enforcement_expected_cap() -> ByteSize {
  gunbc_runner_slot_desired().memory_max
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_oracle_note: String = "WHAT THIS WALL USED TO ASSERT WAS ITS OWN INPUT. Three of its five conjuncts compared gunbc_runner_slot_desired's fields to byte literals retyped from that same declaration twenty lines above, in the same file — measure() == measure() with the equality maintained by hand, which the 2026-08-01 test-oracle ruling names exactly: automate the literal's update and the assertion collapses to nothing, so the manual update WAS the assertion's entire content. It could only ever fail when someone edited one copy and forgot the other, and it would have gone red on this change for that reason and no other.\n\nThe conjuncts that replace them are relations between INDEPENDENTLY derived quantities, so each can fail for a reason worth knowing. The max−1GiB relation is the authored MemoryHigh law from the 2026-07-11 ruling and it is checkable without naming either number. The MemoryHigh ≥ floor conjunct is the load-bearing one and it is NEW: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget is a MEASURED refusal threshold (12GiB, from the doomed-witness receipts below) that claim_executor enforces at arm time, and MemoryHigh is precisely the budget the run-time governor arms against — so shrinking the slot cap below it would ship a fleet on which every floor run refuses itself. Nothing connected those two rows before; the 14GiB ruling is what made the distance between them small enough to matter.\n\nGuaranteed mode is deliberately NOT asserted here any more. runner_slice_cap is derived as slots × MemoryMax, so Σ ≤ cap is true by construction and a check of it would be the vacuous restatement DESIGN 5 says to prefer construction over. gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice survives as the general relation for callers holding two independent numbers."

fn gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_wall_holds() -> Bool {
  let per_slot = gunbc_runner_slot_desired()
  let gibibyte_bytes = 1073741824
  (hardware_thread_count_value(t: gunbc_runner_cores_per_slot()) == 8)
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) + gibibyte_bytes == byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_high) >= byte_size_count(b: gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget))
    && (byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_swap_max) > byte_size_count(b: per_slot.memory_max))
    && all(
      gunbc_committed_runner_slot_allocations(),
      a =>
        a.slots_per_host > 0
          && gunbc_runner_slot_oversubscription_within_slice(
               slots: a.slots_per_host,
               per_slot: a.per_slot.memory_max,
               slice_cap: a.runner_slice_cap
             )
    )
}

data gunbc_runner_slot_allocation_live_cap_dissolve_on: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.runner_slot_allocation",
    decl_name: "gunbc_runner_slot_desired",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

